# Session: 02-kiddo-kdtree (ICP variant 2)

**Scenario:** [`../`](../) — ICP variants comparison
**Approach:** `kiddo` KD-tree for O(log n) nearest-neighbour
**Status:** Skeleton — implements ICP with KD-tree, not benchmarked yet

## What this is

Same ICP algorithm as variant 01, but the nearest-neighbour search uses
the [`kiddo`](https://crates.io/crates/kiddo) KD-tree crate.

- KD-tree built **once** before the iteration loop
- Per-query cost: O(log n) average, O(n) worst case
- Total: O(n log n) per ICP iteration

## Build & test

```powershell
cd experimental/scenarios/2026-08-19-icp-variants/02-kiddo-kdtree
cargo test
cargo run --release --bin icp_bench -- 2000 42
```

## Expected performance

For N points (assumes 50 ICP iterations):

| N | KD-tree build | Time per iteration | Total time |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1,000 | 1 ms | 1 ms | 50 ms |
| 10,000 | 20 ms | 20 ms | 1 s |
| 100,000 | 250 ms | 200 ms | 10 s |
| 1,000,000 | 3 s | 2 s | 2 min |
| 10,000,000 | 40 s | 25 s | 20 min |

**Verdict:** usable for medium surveys (1-10M points), borderline for
large surveys.

## When to use

- Medium point clouds (10k – 10M)
- When you want a low-dependency solution
- When you want a `kiddo` ecosystem (it's also the dependency for
  many other Rust geo crates)

## When NOT to use

- Tiny clouds — overhead of building the tree isn't worth it
- Massive clouds (10M+) — better options exist (octree, GPU KD-tree)
- When you need fuzzy / k-NN queries with custom distance metrics

